  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chilly overnight with a chance for frost in the\nnorth.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nCool airmass moves over northern Maine tonight. There is \na chance for atmospheric decoupling early in the night, as skies\nclear out. This will help temperatures to drop into the 30s. \nPotential for northern areas in Aroostook to drop into the low-\nto-mid 30s, which will lead to some frost development. Frost \nchances are going to be highly dependent on the timing of some \nhigh level clouds moving in late tonight into early \nWednesday morning. If the clouds move in earlier in the night, \ntemperatures will stabilize faster. If clouds move in slower, \nthen there is more time for the temperatures to drop with clear \nskies. Currently, the set up looks more favorable for \ndecoupling, and thus colder temperatures. \n\nThe next chance of frost comes Friday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Soaking rain is expected areawide Wednesday\nnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA large surface low pressure system in north Canada is expected\nto bring a strong warm front early Wednesday night and the cold\nfront quickly exiting the region late Wednesday night. Thus, \nrain should begin early Wednesday evening and taper off around \nsunrise Thursday. The rain will be occasionally moderate, and \ncannot rule out an embedded thunderstorm with some instability \naloft. As of this update, rainfall totals should reach between \n0.5 to 0.75 inches throughout the region. Localized higher \ntotals are possible with heavier convection. Flooding is not a \nconcern with this system, but rain amounts should give some \nrelief to the drought.\n\nAs the system exits on Thursday, some wrap around moisture from\nthe system could bring some isolated showers through the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strong gusty winds expected with the rain\nWednesday night and again during the day Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nAs mentioned above, we are looking at a potent low pressure \nsystem passing to the north of the forecast area with strong \nwinds aloft. This will bring strong gusts in the middle of the \nnight Wednesday night from the south, and again during the day \nThursday from the west. The winds aloft will be the strongest \nWednesday night with up to about 55 kt at 850mb, but it is very \nunlikely that these winds will reach the ground due to stable \nlow levels that are decoupled from the atmosphere aloft. More \nrealistically, we are looking at gusts as high as about 30 mph \nfrom the south Wednesday night in some land areas, with gusts\npotentially up to 35 mph. Over the waters Wednesday night, a \nbit of a low level jet develops just offshore, bringing strong \ngusts, and potentially gales on outer waters. During the day \nThursday, though the winds aloft aren't quite as strong, the \nwinds have a much better chance at mixing down to the ground \nthanks to cold advection aloft with steeper lapse rates, plus \nthe fact that it's daytime. Gusts to about 35 mph from the west \nappear likely Thursday with the potential for up to 40 mph \ngusts, and with wet soils after Wednesday night's rain plus \nleaves on the trees, there could be very isolated tree \ndamage/isolated power outages. Gusts over the waters begin to\ndie down a bit as the low level jet weakens throughout the day\nThursday. It remains breezy Thursday night/Friday, though not \nquite as much as Thursday.",
